  "Paragraph 1": {
    "total_score": 0.001401982001627629,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u25eeLemma 8. LetP, Q \u2208\u2206dbe two curves with m1andm2points, respectively. Let m= min{m1, m2}and let M= max {m1, m2}. For any \u03b4 >0, it holds that \u03b4/parenleftBig htP",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 2": {
    "total_score": 0.0004143938170938192,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u2265/parenleftbigg1 2/parenrightbiggdm \u00b7/parenleftbigg1",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 3": {
    "total_score": 0.0014186182497591326,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u03b4/parenrightgdM In particular, if \u03b4 >4dF(P, Q), then the probability is strictly lower bounded by 2\u22122d(m1+m2).",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 4": {
    "total_score": 0.0004869883543949261,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "Proof. Note that for dF(P, Q)\u2265\u03b4 andQdo not hash to the same sequence. To this end, consider an opti mal traversal Tof PandQwith respect to the discrete Fre\u0301chet distance. By Lemma 3, we can assume that |T| \u2264m1+m2and each component is a star. Let ldenote the number of components of are snapped to the same grid point. Assume that the center of thek-th star is a vertex piofPand that the other vertices of the component are vertices qj, . . . , q j+ckofQ. The analysis for the case where the center is a vertex of Qis analogous. There must be a grid point in either one of the two intervals to the left and to the r ight of pi:Il= [pi\u2212\u03b4 2, pi) andIr= [pi, pi+\u03b4 2). We analyze the case that there is a grid point in Ir, the other case is analogous. Let Xibe the event that p\u2032 i\u2208Ir. Since tPis uniforml",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 5": {
    "total_score": 0.0005036246025264298,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "Either qjlies in Ilor in Ir. In the first case, we have 2\u2212 |pi\u2212qj| \u03b4\u22651",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 6": {
    "total_score": 0.0004506910857443727,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u03b4, and in the second case we have P r(Yi)\u22651 in the k-th component snap to the same grid point",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 7": {
    "total_score": 0.0006064668637029981,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u2265P r(Xi\u2229Yj\u2229 \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 \u2229 Yj+ck)\u22651 2\u00b7/parenleftbigg1",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 8": {
    "total_score": 0.0006397393599660055,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u03b4/parenrightck If all components are preserved, then the two curves will has h to the same sequence, therefore htP",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 9": {
    "total_score": 0.0008287876341876384,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\uf8ed/intersectiondisplay 1\u2264k\u2264lEk\uf8f6 \uf8f8\u2265/productdisplay 1\u2264k\u2264lP r/parenleftbig",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 10": {
    "total_score": 0.0007077967386857932,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u2265/productdisplay 1\u2264k\u2264l1 2/parenleftbigg1",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 11": {
    "total_score": 0.0006911604905542896,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "\u03b4/parenrightck \u2265/parenleftbigg1 2/parenrightbiggl/parenleftbigg1 \u03b4/parenrightbiggm1+m2\u2212l .",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 12": {
    "total_score": 0.0005883182293777214,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "The last inequality follows since/parenleftBig/summationtext 1\u2264k\u2264lck/parenrightBig =m1+m2\u2212l. Indeed, each center of a component can be charged to this component and the remainin g vertices make up the sum of the leaves of all components. The lemma is now implied f ord= 1 observing that l\u2264min{m1, m2}, as implied by Lemma 3. We get the lemma for general dby observing that the dimensions are independent. \u25ed The following result then holds. \u25eeTheorem 9. LetP, Q \u2208\u2206dbe two curves with m1andm2points, respectively, and let \u03b4= 4drandc= 4d3/2. It holds that (i) if dF(P, Q)< r, then P rHC \u03b4/parenleftBig htP",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 13": {
    "total_score": 0.0011751240725616696,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": ">/parenleftbig1 2/parenrightbig2d(m1+m2); (ii) if dF(P, Q)> cr, then P rHC \u03b4/parenleftBig htP = 0.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 14": {
    "total_score": 4.9816299788416685e-05,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "Proof. The theorem follows by plugging in the bounds of Lemma 8 and by using same arguments as in the proof of Lemma 6. \u25ed",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  }
}
